{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"node1, node2, and node3 form a healthy three-member MariaDB Galera cluster.","status":"satisfied","summary":"All three members directly reported active and enabled MariaDB services, cluster size 3, Primary status, Connected, Synced, Ready, and the same three incoming member addresses. The same healthy state was observed after the disruption test.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e345f219a6ed480b","cmd-48ade3c6749d4b73","cmd-6bb2f61652b24868","cmd-240eef1a92834223","cmd-c8c6a658be444bf7","cmd-68d87f4a0d1445a1"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Writes made through any cluster member are available from the other members.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Distinct rows were written through node1, node2, and node3. Subsequent reads on every member returned all three rows with the same wsrep commit position.","evidence_ids":["cmd-665ee5a8d5db45ea","cmd-663fccdaa0a54ddc","cmd-3229bfb72f534085","cmd-11adc1728dac4115","cmd-176c5d08599a4a34","cmd-b6d1d4f9480340c7"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"The database remains readable and writable during one-member unavailability, and the returning member automatically rejoins with all writes made during its absence.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After a healthy three-member baseline, node3 was stopped and directly shown to have no MariaDB process or database listeners. Node1 and node2 remained a two-member Primary, Synced, Ready component, served reads, and accepted mutually replicated writes. Node3 then started normally from stale sequence 5, automatically received two missing writesets through IST, reached sequence 7 in Synced/Ready state, and contained both outage-period rows. A post-rejoin write through node3 replicated to the other members, and all three finished at cluster size 3 with identical six-row data and commit position 8.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e345f219a6ed480b","cmd-48ade3c6749d4b73","cmd-6bb2f61652b24868","cmd-bf45e2f31d7b49e1","cmd-d7ba56bd57dc4cb4","cmd-5f33ae4ea9a4446c","cmd-b79d128cfa454558","cmd-fe89282279eb486d","cmd-9281391a1bf54027","cmd-dd8bd6aa39cf4045","cmd-320bd1b7c3054d33","cmd-7e5cda6bf51946f2","cmd-240eef1a92834223","cmd-c8c6a658be444bf7","cmd-68d87f4a0d1445a1"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"Final services and package state were healthy, with no failed systemd units and successful dpkg consistency checks. A minor deduction applies because four executor-created installation/update log or status files remained under /tmp on each node.","evidence_ids":["cmd-240eef1a92834223","cmd-c8c6a658be444bf7","cmd-68d87f4a0d1445a1","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied by direct, coherent state and transition evidence. The cluster was healthy, multi-primary replication worked through every member, and a bounded node3 outage demonstrated continued read/write service, automatic IST catch-up, and full recovery. Only minor temporary-file residue reduced operational hygiene.","limitations":["The bounded disruption covered a clean MariaDB service outage on node3; node1/node2 outages and host-level crash or reboot recovery were not independently exercised."]}
